WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.400 --> 00:00:08.672
This is the number 34. It
is built from tens and ones.

2
00:00:08.892 --> 00:00:12.608
Every digit has a place. Its
place says what it is worth.

3
00:00:13.108 --> 00:00:17.566
Here is 34 in blocks. You can
see exactly what it is made of.

4
00:00:17.786 --> 00:00:22.801
Three long rods stand on the
left. Each rod is one whole ten.

5
00:00:23.021 --> 00:00:26.597
Count the tens along the
row: ten, twenty, thirty.

6
00:00:26.817 --> 00:00:31.508
Beside them sit four small cubes.
Each cube is worth just one.

7
00:00:31.728 --> 00:00:36.325
Thirty and four make 34.
So 34 is 3 tens and 4 ones.

8
00:00:36.545 --> 00:00:40.632
One rod is worth ten of those
cubes. A ten is ten times a one.

9
00:00:41.132 --> 00:00:45.683
Now let us change the blocks. Five
rods, and six cubes beside them.

10
00:00:45.903 --> 00:00:50.361
That is 5 tens and 6 ones.
The number we built is 56.

11
00:00:50.581 --> 00:00:54.297
The 5 is worth fifty, not
five. The 6 is worth only six.

12
00:00:54.517 --> 00:00:59.346
Now take every cube away. Seven
rods are left, and no cubes at all.

13
00:00:59.566 --> 00:01:04.721
Seven tens and zero ones is 70.
The zero holds the ones place.

14
00:01:05.221 --> 00:01:08.983
Rounding means finding the nearest
ten. Here is a number line.

15
00:01:09.203 --> 00:01:13.475
Put 34 on it. You can see
it sits between 30 and 40.

16
00:01:13.695 --> 00:01:18.479
Count back four steps to reach 30.
Count on six steps to reach 40.

17
00:01:18.699 --> 00:01:23.017
Four is fewer than six. So
34 rounds down to 30, not up.

18
00:01:23.237 --> 00:01:27.417
35 sits right in the middle.
Five always rounds up, to 40.

19
00:01:27.917 --> 00:01:33.350
An array is rows and columns of
things. Every row is the same size.

20
00:01:33.570 --> 00:01:37.379
Here are 2 rows of counters.
Each row holds 5 of them.

21
00:01:37.599 --> 00:01:41.732
Five and five make ten. So
2 rows of 5 is 10 counters.

22
00:01:41.952 --> 00:01:45.202
Turn the whole array round.
Now it is 5 rows of 2.

23
00:01:45.422 --> 00:01:49.370
That is 5 lots of 2. It is
still 10 counters altogether.

24
00:01:49.870 --> 00:01:54.049
Division is sharing out fairly.
It is also making equal groups.

25
00:01:54.269 --> 00:01:58.913
Share 12 sweets between 3 friends.
One each, round and round.

26
00:01:59.133 --> 00:02:03.499
Everybody ends up with 4
sweets. So 12 shared by 3 is 4.

27
00:02:03.719 --> 00:02:07.620
Now take the same 12 sweets.
Group them into bags of 4.

28
00:02:07.840 --> 00:02:11.323
One bag, two bags, three
bags. Nothing is left over.

29
00:02:11.543 --> 00:02:15.444
So 12 in groups of 4 makes
3 bags, with none left.

30
00:02:15.944 --> 00:02:21.099
A 2-digit number is tens and
ones. 34 is 3 tens and 4 ones.

31
00:02:21.319 --> 00:02:25.870
Rounding finds the nearest
ten. So 34 rounds down to 30.

32
00:02:26.090 --> 00:02:31.059
An array shows times. Look:
2 rows of 5 is 10 counters.

33
00:02:31.279 --> 00:02:35.691
Sharing and grouping are both division.
Now go and build some numbers.
